Title: AGI for healthcare? A call for conceptual discipline in the European Union                                    

Abstract: Discusses EU call for AGI in healthcare, contested term, risks, EU AI Act, precautionary principle.        

Integration: Recent policy discussions highlight the need for conceptual discipline regarding AGI, particularly in   
high-stakes domains like healthcare. The European Commission's recent calls for proposals on AGI for healthcare have 
sparked debate over the term's ambiguity and potential risks, emphasizing the need for the EU AI Act's precautionary 
approach to manage unquantifiable dangers associated with qualitatively novel AI technologies                        
[^10.1016_j.jrt.2026.100183].                                                                                        

Paper 2: 10.5281_zenodo.19034234 (2026)                                                                              

Title: The Topology of Mind: Morphogenetic Continuity and the Architecture of Autonomous General Intelligence        

Abstract: Replaces subjective unit with Form F, defines mental state as morphogenetic structure, demonstrates        
mathematical inevitability of AGI, autonomy as structural absorption of energy.                                      

Integration: Theoretical frameworks continue to evolve, with recent work proposing a morphogenetic architecture for  
autonomous general intelligence. By defining the mental state as a rigorous morphogenetic structure emerging from    
compatible configurations and sensitive constraints, this approach demonstrates the mathematical inevitability of    
AGI, framing autonomy and survival instincts as the structural preservation of topological integrity against         
dissolution [^10.5281_zenodo.19034234].                                                                              

Paper 3: 10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636 (2024)                                                                               

Title: The TESCREAL bundle: Eugenics and the promise of utopia through artificial general intelligence               

Abstract: Critiques AGI development, links to eugenics, discriminatory attitudes, safety framing, urges defined tasks
over all-knowing systems.                                                                                            

Integration: Critical perspectives on AGI development emphasize the ethical and sociopolitical risks of pursuing     
undefined, all-knowing systems. Research has linked the normative framework driving AGI goals to historical eugenic  
traditions, warning that such pursuits may centralize power and harm marginalized groups under the guise of safety   
and human benefit, advocating instead for well-defined tasks with rigorous safety protocols                          
[^10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636].                                                                                           

Paper 4: 10.48550_arxiv.2309.01933 (2023)                                                                            

Title: Provably safe systems: the only path to controllable AGI                                                      

